### What are suppressed exceptions?

Suppressed exceptions are secondary exceptions that occur during resource cleanup in try-with-resources. They are attached to the primary exception (from the try body) rather than replacing it.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class SuppressedExceptionDemo {

    static class TradeConnection implements AutoCloseable {
        @Override
        public void close() {
            throw new RuntimeException("CLOSE_FAILED: connection reset");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (TradeConnection conn = new TradeConnection()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("TRADE_FAILED: invalid exchange rate");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Primary: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Suppressed count: " + e.getSuppressed().length);
            for (Throwable suppressed : e.getSuppressed()) {
                System.out.println("Suppressed: " + suppressed.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Primary: TRADE_FAILED: invalid exchange rate
Suppressed count: 1
Suppressed: CLOSE_FAILED: connection reset
```

### How do multiple suppressed exceptions work?

When multiple resources each fail on close, each failure is added as a separate suppressed exception.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class MultipleSuppressedDemo {

    static class Resource implements AutoCloseable {
        private final String name;
        Resource(String name) { this.name = name; }
        @Override public void close() {
            throw new RuntimeException("CLOSE_" + name);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Resource r1 = new Resource("CONN");
             Resource r2 = new Resource("STMT");
             Resource r3 = new Resource("RS")) {
            throw new RuntimeException("MAIN_ERROR: settlement failed");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Primary: " + e.getMessage());
            for (Throwable s : e.getSuppressed()) {
                System.out.println("Suppressed: " + s.getMessage());
            }
            // Closed in reverse: RS, STMT, CONN
            // Each close failure is a separate suppressed exception
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Primary: MAIN_ERROR: settlement failed
Suppressed: CLOSE_RS
Suppressed: CLOSE_STMT
Suppressed: CLOSE_CONN
```

### How is this better than the pre-Java-7 finally pattern?

In the old pattern, a throw in finally completely replaces the try exception. The real error is gone.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class OldVsNewComparison {

    // OLD: finally throw REPLACES try exception
    public static void oldPattern() {
        try {
            throw new RuntimeException("TRADE_FAILED");
        } finally {
            throw new RuntimeException("CLOSE_FAILED");
        }
        // Caller sees ONLY: CLOSE_FAILED
        // TRADE_FAILED is GONE. Not suppressed. LOST.
    }

    // NEW: try-with-resources preserves BOTH
    public static void newPattern() {
        try (AutoCloseable resource = () -> {
            throw new RuntimeException("CLOSE_FAILED");
        }) {
            throw new RuntimeException("TRADE_FAILED");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Primary: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Suppressed: " + e.getSuppressed()[0].getMessage());
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("=== Old pattern ===");
        try {
            oldPattern();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Caller sees: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("TRADE_FAILED is LOST\n");
        }

        System.out.println("=== New pattern ===");
        newPattern();
    }
}
```

```text
=== Old pattern ===
Caller sees: CLOSE_FAILED
TRADE_FAILED is LOST

=== New pattern ===
Primary: TRADE_FAILED
Suppressed: CLOSE_FAILED
```

### How do you manually add suppressed exceptions?

You can call `addSuppressed()` in your own code. This is useful for batch processing where you want to collect all errors into one exception.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class BatchSettlementService {

    public static void settleBatch(List<String> tradeIds) {
        RuntimeException primary = null;

        for (String tradeId : tradeIds) {
            try {
                settle(tradeId);
                System.out.println("Settled: " + tradeId);
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                if (primary == null) {
                    primary = e;  // first error becomes primary
                } else {
                    primary.addSuppressed(e);  // subsequent errors are suppressed
                }
            }
        }

        if (primary != null) {
            throw primary;  // one exception with ALL failures attached
        }
    }

    private static void settle(String tradeId) {
        if (tradeId.contains("BAD")) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Settlement failed for " + tradeId);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            settleBatch(List.of("TRD-001", "TRD-BAD-002", "TRD-003", "TRD-BAD-004"));
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("\nPrimary: " + e.getMessage());
            for (Throwable s : e.getSuppressed()) {
                System.out.println("Suppressed: " + s.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Settled: TRD-001
Settled: TRD-003

Primary: Settlement failed for TRD-BAD-002
Suppressed: Settlement failed for TRD-BAD-004
```

### What does the stack trace look like with suppressed exceptions?

Logging frameworks like SLF4J and Logback automatically include suppressed exceptions in the output.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class SuppressedStackTraceDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (AutoCloseable r = () -> { throw new Exception("CLOSE_ERROR"); }) {
            throw new RuntimeException("MAIN_ERROR");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
            // Output includes:
            // java.lang.RuntimeException: MAIN_ERROR
            //   at ...
            //   Suppressed: java.lang.Exception: CLOSE_ERROR
            //     at ...
        }
    }
}
```

```text
java.lang.RuntimeException: MAIN_ERROR
	at com.bank.demo.SuppressedStackTraceDemo.main(SuppressedStackTraceDemo.java:6)
	Suppressed: java.lang.Exception: CLOSE_ERROR
		at com.bank.demo.SuppressedStackTraceDemo.lambda$main$0(SuppressedStackTraceDemo.java:5)
```

### What if only close() throws but try succeeds?

If the try body completes normally and close() throws, the close exception becomes the primary (not suppressed) because there is no try-body exception to attach it to.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class CloseOnlyThrowsDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (AutoCloseable r = () -> { throw new Exception("Close failed"); }) {
            System.out.println("Try body succeeded");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Exception from close: " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Suppressed count: " + e.getSuppressed().length);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Try body succeeded
Exception from close: Close failed
Suppressed count: 0
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Throwable.addSuppressed() was added in Java 7** specifically for try-with-resources, but it is a public method you can use anywhere.
- **Performance.** addSuppressed() is not free. Each call allocates or resizes an internal array. For normal usage (a few suppressed per exception), this is negligible.
- **Serialization.** Suppressed exceptions are serialized along with the primary exception. They survive RPC boundaries and log aggregation.
- **Log aggregation.** ELK Stack, Splunk, and CloudWatch all display suppressed exceptions from the stack trace. No special configuration needed.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the difference between suppressed and caused-by? (Suppressed is for concurrent exceptions from close(). Caused-by is for exception chaining/wrapping.)
- Can you have both suppressed and caused-by? (Yes. A primary exception can have a cause AND suppressed exceptions.)
- What happens if addSuppressed is called with the exception itself? (`e.addSuppressed(e)` throws IllegalArgumentException.)
- Is suppressed transitive? (No. Each exception has its own getSuppressed() array.)
